  <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6 mb-8">
    <h2>1. The Disaster of "Three-Sided Adhesion"</h2>
    <p>
      An elastomeric sealant (such as polyurethane or silicone) functions like a rubber band: it must stretch horizontally as concrete slabs shrink during sub-zero winter freezes, and compress as slabs expand under scorching summer heat.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you dispense sealant into a clean concrete joint without backer rod, the sealant bonds to <strong>three surfaces</strong>: the left concrete wall, the right concrete wall, and the bottom subgrade.
    </p>
    <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-600 p-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-blue-900 my-4">
      When winter cold forces the two concrete slabs to pull apart, the bottom-bonded center of the sealant cannot move. Immense shear stress concentrates directly at the lower corners, causing the sealant to tear lengthwise right down the center—a catastrophic failure known as <strong>cohesive failure</strong>.
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>The Backer Rod as a Bond Breaker:</strong> Polyurethane and silicone sealants physically cannot adhere to polyethylene foam. The backer rod acts as a mechanical bond-breaker, restricting adhesion strictly to the two opposing concrete sidewalls. The sealant forms a flexible <strong>hourglass shape</strong> that stretches evenly across its entire cross-section.
    </p>

    <h2>2. The ACI 224.3R 2:1 Width-to-Depth Ratio</h2>
    <p>
      Per American Concrete Institute (ACI 224.3R) and ASTM C1193, elastomeric joint sealant must be installed according to strict geometric proportions:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Joints up to 1/2" Wide:</strong> The depth of the sealant must equal <strong>half the width (2:1 ratio)</strong>. A 1/2-inch wide joint must have a sealant depth of exactly 1/4 inch. (Minimum allowable depth is 1/4 inch).</li>
      <li><strong>Joints between 1/2" and 1" Wide:</strong> The depth of the sealant should remain between <strong>1/4 inch and 3/8 inch</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Joints over 1" Wide (up to 2"):</strong> The depth of the sealant should <strong>never exceed 1/2 inch</strong>, regardless of width. Deeper sealant increases internal tension stresses beyond the adhesive bond strength.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Backer Rod Sizing &amp; Depth Reference Table</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-700 uppercase font-semibold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Concrete Joint Width</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Recommended Backer Rod Diameter</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Target Sealant Depth (Top of Rod to Surface)</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Recess from Concrete Face</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
