            <td class="p-3 text-purple-700 font-bold">1-1/4" Closed-Cell Rod</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-purple-700 font-bold">1/2 Inch (Max Depth)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-purple-700 font-bold">1/8 to 1/4 Inch Recessed</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>4. Closed-Cell vs. Open-Cell Foam: Why Driveways Require Closed-Cell</h2>
    <p>
      Backer rods are manufactured in two primary formulations with completely different physical properties:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Closed-Cell Polyethylene Foam:</strong> Made of non-absorbent, extruded round polyethylene with an impermeable outer skin. It absorbs 0% water. If water ever gets past the top seal, closed-cell rod will not wick water downward into the subgrade. It is mandatory for horizontal exterior pavements, driveways, patios, and wet areas.</li>
      <li><strong>Open-Cell Polyurethane Foam:</strong> An open honeycomb sponge material. It compresses easily into irregular gaps, but acts like a sponge, soaking up water and holding trapped moisture against concrete sidewalls, causing freeze-thaw spalling. Open-cell is strictly limited to vertical interior building expansion joints.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Trade Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    expertName="David Miller, PE"
    role="Senior Structural & Geotechnical Engineer"
    experience="25+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-blue-100 text-blue-800"
    initials="DM"
    title="Never Puncture Closed-Cell Backer Rod with Screwdrivers or Trowels"
    note="When inserting backer rod into a tight concrete joint, DIYers frequently use a flathead screwdriver or pointed putty knife to jam the foam down. DO NOT DO THIS! Closed-cell backer rod contains millions of sealed cells filled with trapped blowing gas. If you puncture or crease the skin of the rod, that trapped gas outgasses through the puncture hole. When you pour liquid polyurethane sealant over the punctured rod, the escaping gas creates massive bubbling blisters—transforming your expensive sealant into a hollow, honeycomb sponge! Always install backer rod using a smooth, blunt tool: a wooden paint stir stick with rounded edges, a plastic spoon, or an inexpensive roller wheel backer rod insertion tool."
  />

